    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder With Fiber</Fragment>
      Premier Protein Plus Fiber, Happy Viking, Orgain, Ka'Chava, Huel and Garden of Life compared on the two numbers that matter: how many grams of fiber you actually get per serving, and which kind of fiber it is.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#why-fiber">Why Add Fiber</a>
      <a href="#how-much">How Much Is Meaningful</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#fiber-types">Inulin, Acacia, Psyllium</a>
      <a href="#bloating">Gas and Bloating</a>
      <a href="#satiety-glucose">Fullness and Blood Sugar</a>
      <a href="#compare">Side-by-Side Numbers</a>
      <a href="#diy">Add Your Own Psyllium</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#how-we-chose">How We Chose</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Most protein powders contain one gram of fiber or less, so a scoop that brings 5-10 g is doing something genuinely different. That extra fiber can make a shake more filling, blunt the rise in blood sugar from whatever you blend it with, and close part of a gap most adults have: the target is 25-38 g a day and the typical intake is far lower. But the type of fiber matters as much as the grams. Inulin and chicory root can leave some people bloated for hours, while acacia and partially hydrolyzed guar gum tend to be gentler. Below are six powders with a meaningful dose of fiber on the label, what each fiber source is likely to do in your gut, and when you are better off buying a plain protein powder and adding a spoon of psyllium yourself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-fiber" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Put Fiber in a Protein Shake
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fiber is the nutrient most adults are shortest on. US dietary guidance sets adequate intake at about 14 g per 1,000 calories, which works out to roughly 25 g a day for many women and 38 g for many men, and national survey data has long shown that the large majority of Americans fall well short of it. A typical day of eating lands somewhere in the mid-teens. A protein shake that brings 5-10 g is therefore not a gimmick: it can cover a quarter or more of the gap with no extra effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein powders are usually almost fiber-free because the manufacturing process strips it out. Whey is filtered from milk, which has no fiber to begin with, and pea or rice protein isolates are concentrated precisely by removing starch and fiber. That is why a standard whey tub reads 0-1 g per scoop. Anything above 3 g means the brand has either added a fiber ingredient or built the formula around whole seeds, grains and legumes, and the label will tell you which.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for combining the two is mostly practical. Protein and fiber are the two nutrients that do the most for fullness per calorie, so a shake with both tends to hold you longer than one with protein alone. That matters if you use the shake as a breakfast, a snack between meals or part of a calorie deficit. It also matters for blood sugar, because certain fibers slow how quickly carbohydrate from the fruit, milk or oats in your blender reaches your bloodstream.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a trade-off. Adding fiber makes a shake thicker, can change the taste, and in some people causes gas, bloating or looser stools, especially if the fiber is inulin or chicory root and the person is not used to it. A high-fiber powder is a useful tool for people who eat little produce, but it is not automatically better than a plain protein powder for someone who already eats beans, oats, vegetables and fruit every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also does not replace food. Whole plant foods bring fiber in a matrix with water, potassium, polyphenols and a range of fiber types that no single additive reproduces. Think of a fiber-boosted shake as a reliable top-up on days when your diet is thin, not as a reason to skip the vegetables. Everything below assumes that framing, and none of it is medical advice; anyone with a digestive condition should run changes past a doctor or registered dietitian.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Fiber per Scoop Is Meaningful
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against a 25-38 g daily target, the useful threshold for a protein powder is about 5 g per serving. Below that, the fiber is incidental: a gram or two from cocoa, gums or a pinch of chicory that the brand may still advertise on the front of the tub. At 5 g you are covering roughly 15-20 percent of a typical target. At 7-10 g, which is where the stronger products on this list sit, a single shake does as much as a large bowl of oatmeal or a cup of cooked lentils.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Fiber in Context</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25-38 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily target for most adults</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0-1 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical whey scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5 g+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Meaningful per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most here (Happy Viking)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Watch the serving size behind the number. A powder that lists 10 g of fiber in a 63 g, 250-calorie serving is a different product from one with 7 g in a 41 g, 130-calorie serving. The first is a light meal; the second is a protein shake that happens to carry fiber. Dividing fiber by calories is a quick way to compare: Premier Protein Plus Fiber gives about 5 g per 100 calories, Happy Viking about 4 g, and the meal-style shakes from Huel and Ka'Chava closer to 2-2.5 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            More is not always better in a single sitting. Going from 1 g to 10 g of fiber in one drink, with no build-up, is exactly how people end up uncomfortable and conclude that the powder does not agree with them. Spreading fiber across the day, with one shake plus fiber from meals, tends to be better tolerated than loading it into one glass. If you already eat 25 g or more from food, a 5-7 g shake is plenty and a 10 g one may push you past what your gut is used to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, remember that labels list total dietary fiber and rarely break it down. Two powders can both say 7 g while one is mostly fermentable inulin and the other is mostly acacia and guar. That is why the next section matters more than the headline number: the ingredient list, not the nutrition panel, tells you what kind of fiber you are buying and how your gut is likely to react.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Protein Powders With Fiber
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on the label, not on taste tests: grams of fiber per serving, the fiber source, protein per serving, sugar and price where we could check it. We did not test these products hands-on. Figures are per the label or the brand's listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the panel on the tub you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="fiber-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Inulin, Acacia, Psyllium and the Rest
          </h2>

          <p>
            Inulin and chicory root fiber are the most common added fibers in protein products because they are cheap, nearly tasteless and slightly sweet. Chemically they are fructans, chains of fructose that your small intestine cannot digest, so they arrive in the colon intact and are fermented quickly by gut bacteria. That fermentation is the prebiotic effect brands advertise, and it can feed beneficial bacteria. It is also what produces gas. Agave inulin, which Orgain uses, is the same family.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acacia fiber, also listed as acacia gum or gum arabic, is fermented much more slowly and further along the colon, which is why it tends to cause less gas at the same dose. Partially hydrolyzed guar gum, or PHGG, is similar in behavior and is one of the few added fibers that has been studied as tolerable for many people following a low-FODMAP approach. Premier Protein Plus Fiber combines both with soluble corn fiber, which is also generally well tolerated at moderate doses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Psyllium husk is the odd one out, and arguably the most useful. It forms a thick gel in water, which is what gives it its effects on stool form, fullness and blood sugar, and it is only lightly fermented, so it tends to produce less gas than inulin. Psyllium soluble fiber carries an FDA-authorized health claim related to heart disease risk as part of a diet low in saturated fat and cholesterol. Few protein powders contain it because the gel makes shakes set solid if they sit, which is one reason adding your own often makes more sense.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
