          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar separates them too. Premier lists 1 g of sugar with none added and Garden of Life 0 g, while Happy Viking lists 5 g and Ka'Chava 7 g, partly from cane sugar and coconut nectar. For most people a few grams of sugar inside a 250-calorie meal is unremarkable, but it matters if you are managing blood sugar or trying to keep a snack shake as lean as possible. Sweetener choice matters to some buyers as well: Premier uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium, Orgain stevia, Garden of Life erythritol and stevia.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price per serving varies more than any other number. At prices we saw, Garden of Life worked out to about 1.80 dollars a serving, Happy Viking about 4 dollars at the brand's list price, and Ka'Chava about 5.50 dollars at a retailer for a seven-serving bag. Huel Black Edition sits between, and Premier is typically the cheapest whey option with added fiber at mainstream grocery stores. Prices move constantly, so compare per serving on the day you buy, not per tub.
          </p>
