        <div id="diy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Cheaper Route: Add Your Own Psyllium
          </h2>

          <p>
            For many people the best protein powder with fiber is a good plain protein powder plus a spoonful of psyllium husk. It is usually cheaper, it lets you choose your fiber type rather than accepting the brand's inulin, and it lets you adjust the dose to what your gut tolerates. A single tub of psyllium husk powder lasts months, and you can keep using whatever protein you already like for taste and price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Psyllium husk powder supplies roughly 3-5 g of fiber per rounded teaspoon depending on the product, so check the label on the tub you buy. Start with half a teaspoon in your shake for the first week, then build toward one or two teaspoons if it agrees with you. Mix it in last, drink the shake straight away, and add an extra glass of water, because psyllium keeps absorbing liquid and will turn a shake into something closer to pudding if it sits for ten minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other add-ins work too. Ground flaxseed and chia each bring several grams of fiber per tablespoon along with omega-3 fat, though they add calories. Rolled oats blended into a shake add beta-glucan, a viscous fiber with good evidence for cholesterol and glucose. Acacia fiber powder is nearly tasteless and dissolves cleanly, making it the easiest unflavored option if psyllium's texture bothers you. Frozen raspberries or blackberries are among the highest-fiber fruits and improve flavor at the same time.
          </p>

          <p>
            The do-it-yourself route also solves the protein-quality question. Most fiber-boosted powders give up some protein per scoop to make room for the fiber, and Premier's fiber version carries 5 g less protein than its classic powder. If you want 25-30 g of protein from a clean whey isolate or a third-party tested plant blend, buy that, and treat fiber as a separate ingredient. It is the same logic that makes many buyers pair a plain powder with a separate greens product; our <a href="/best-greens-protein-powder/">greens protein powder guide</a> covers the all-in-one alternative.
          </p>

          <p>
            A fiber-boosted powder still wins on convenience. If you mix shakes at work, travel, or simply will not keep a second tub around, a powder with 7 g already in it is the one you will actually use. The best choice is the one that gets fiber into your day consistently, and for plenty of people that means one scoop of a product like Premier Protein Plus Fiber rather than a theoretically better two-ingredient routine that lasts a week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip a High-Fiber Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already eat 25-38 g of fiber a day from beans, whole grains, vegetables and fruit, a fiber-boosted powder adds little and may push you into discomfort. You are better served by a plain protein powder chosen on protein per calorie, testing and taste. Fiber has no extra benefit once you are comfortably meeting the target, and very high intakes can cause bloating and interfere with mineral absorption in some people.
          </p>

          <p>
            People with certain digestive conditions should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first. That includes anyone with a history of bowel obstruction or narrowing, gastroparesis, active inflammatory bowel disease flares, or who has been advised to follow a low-residue diet. People with IBS are not barred from fiber, and soluble fiber such as psyllium is often recommended, but the type matters and fructan-heavy powders are a common trigger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Anyone on medication should also take care. Fiber, and psyllium especially, can slow or reduce absorption of some drugs, which is why fiber supplements are usually taken a couple of hours apart from other medicines. People taking diabetes medication may see their glucose responses change if they add a high-fiber shake daily. People with kidney disease should check with their care team about protein intake and about any mineral premix in meal shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recovering from surgery or adjusting to a new diet are other times to keep it simple. After bariatric surgery, for example, care teams typically introduce protein and fiber in a specific order, and a high-fiber meal shake may not fit the plan. Pregnant and breastfeeding readers can generally use fiber-rich powders, but the superfood, herb and adaptogen blends in some meal shakes, including ashwagandha in Happy Viking, are worth running past a clinician.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip these if what you want is a straightforward muscle-building shake. A post-workout protein shake benefits from fast digestion, and fiber slows things down slightly. The effect on muscle growth is unlikely to matter for most people, since total daily protein is what counts, but if you just want 25-30 g of protein with as few other ingredients as possible, a plain whey isolate or pea protein will do the job more cheaply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How We Chose These Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from products the site already tracks and the label database we maintain, then looked for powders with at least 5 g of fiber per serving on the current panel. That threshold ruled out most protein-first powders, including many that put fiber or prebiotic on the front of the tub but contain only 1-2 g. We checked fiber figures against the brand's product page or a retailer's nutrition panel and noted where they vary by flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within that group, we weighed fiber per serving, the fiber source and its likely tolerability, protein per serving, sugar, sweeteners and price where we could see one. We favored products whose fiber comes from acacia, PHGG, soluble corn fiber or whole foods over those built mainly on inulin, but we included Orgain and Ka'Chava because they are widely bought, carry useful amounts, and suit people who tolerate fructans well.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not test these products hands-on, and we do not report taste or mixability as our own experience. Where we mention texture or flavor, it reflects patterns in buyer reviews. Nutrition figures are per the label or retailer listing at the time of writing; brands revise formulas and panels differ between flavors, so check the tub you buy. Prices are snapshots and change frequently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our full methodology, including how we handle certifications and sourcing, is on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page. Brand-level detail on several of these picks is in our reviews of <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein</a>, <a href="/huel-protein-powder-review/">Huel</a>, <a href="/orgain-protein-powder-review/">Orgain</a> and <a href="/kachava-review/">Ka'Chava</a>. Nothing here is medical advice, and readers with digestive conditions, kidney disease, pregnancy or medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a high-fiber shake.
          </p>
