        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Word Fiber on the Front</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many tubs advertise fiber or prebiotics on the front and deliver only 1-2 g per scoop, which barely moves a 25-38 g daily target. Turn the tub over and read the nutrition panel. Look for at least 5 g per serving, and check the serving size behind it, since a bigger scoop can make a modest product look generous.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Jumping Straight to a Full Serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Going from almost no fiber to 7-10 g in one shake is the fastest route to gas and bloating. Start with half a serving for the first week, drink extra water, and build up as your gut bacteria adapt. Most people who give it two or three weeks find the discomfort settles considerably.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Which Fiber It Is</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Total fiber hides big differences. Inulin, chicory root and agave inulin ferment quickly and are common IBS triggers, while acacia, PHGG and psyllium tend to be gentler. If one fiber powder bloated you, do not write them all off; read the ingredient list and try a product built on a different fiber source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Meal Shake as a Snack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Huel Black Edition, Ka'Chava and Happy Viking carry 240-400 calories a serving. That is fine when the shake replaces breakfast or lunch, but drinking one on top of normal meals adds a meal's worth of energy. If you want fiber in a snack shake, choose a 130-150 calorie powder instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving a Psyllium Shake to Sit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Psyllium keeps absorbing water and will thicken a shake into something like pudding within minutes. Stir it in last and drink it straight away, with an extra glass of water. And take psyllium a couple of hours apart from medications, since it can slow how quickly some of them are absorbed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

