        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder with fiber?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Premier Protein Plus Fiber, because it gives 25 g of whey protein and 7 g of fiber in about 130 calories, per Premier's label, using acacia, PHGG and soluble corn fiber, which tend to be gentler than inulin. Happy Viking has the most fiber, at 10 g, but it is a 250-calorie meal shake. Orgain is the mainstream organic plant option.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much fiber should a protein powder have?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 5 g per serving is the point where a powder makes a meaningful dent in a 25-38 g daily target. Many tubs advertising fiber contain only 1-2 g. The stronger products here give 7-10 g. Compare fiber per calorie as well as per scoop, because meal shakes bring more total fiber but also many more calories.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does fiber in protein powder cause bloating?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can, especially if your usual diet is low in fiber or the fiber is inulin or chicory root, which ferment quickly. Starting with half a serving, drinking extra water and building up over two or three weeks usually helps. If bloating persists, try a powder with acacia, PHGG or psyllium, or a plain powder with no added fiber.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is it better to add psyllium to my protein shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Often, yes. A plain protein powder plus half to two teaspoons of psyllium husk lets you choose a well-studied, less gassy fiber and adjust the dose. It is usually cheaper too. Drink it immediately, because psyllium gels as it sits, and take it a couple of hours apart from medicines, since it can affect their absorption.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is protein powder with fiber good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help, because protein and fiber are the two most filling nutrients per calorie, so a shake with both tends to keep you satisfied longer. The calories still count. A 130-calorie powder with 7 g of fiber is a strong snack, while a 250-400 calorie meal shake only helps if it genuinely replaces a meal rather than adding to one.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does fiber in a shake lower blood sugar spikes?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Viscous fibers such as psyllium, guar and oat beta-glucan have the best evidence for blunting the glucose rise after a meal. Inulin and soluble corn fiber count toward your daily fiber but do little to the glucose curve directly. Protein itself also helps. People on diabetes medication should talk to their care team before adding a daily high-fiber shake.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I get all my fiber from protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No, and you should not try. Even the highest-fiber powder here supplies about 10 g, and whole foods bring a broader mix of fiber types plus vitamins, minerals and polyphenols that powders do not replace. Use a fiber-rich shake as a top-up on days your diet is thin, and keep beans, oats, vegetables, fruit and whole grains as the foundation.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

